SQLUI(1)                       P32W Unix                        SQLUI(1)

NAME
       sqlui - a full-screen interface to a SQL database

SYNOPSIS
       sqlui [-c] dbdir

DESCRIPTION
       sqlui shows the database dbdir (a directory of tables made by
       sqldb(3), as sql(1) uses) on the whole screen of a VT100-like
       terminal, in the spirit of dBASE's ASSIST and BROWSE: a list
       of the tables, a table's rows as a grid, a form to edit or add
       one record, and a line to type any SQL statement on. ? shows
       the keys of the screen it is typed on.

       The last line but one is the status line: the database, the
       screen, where you are (row 5 of 100), and the last message,
       such as an error from the database or 1 row changed. The last
       line lists the keys of the screen, or is where something is
       typed: a filter, an order, a statement, a y or n.

       The tables screen lists the tables with their numbers of rows.
       From it a table can be browsed, described (its columns, types,
       keys and indexes), made (n: type the rest of a CREATE TABLE
       statement) or dropped (x, after asking).

       The browse screen shows the rows of a table under a header of
       column names, the current row in reverse video. A column is as
       wide as its widest value seen so far, up to 30 characters; a
       wider value ends with > (the form shows all of it). INTEGER
       values are on the right, NULL shows as NULL. Columns that do
       not fit scroll sideways. Rows are read a screenful at a time
       (SELECT ... LIMIT n OFFSET m), so a table of any size can be
       browsed; a filter is a WHERE condition and an order an ORDER
       BY list, both typed in SQL.

       The record form has a line per column: its name, its type
       (key for the PRIMARY KEY, req for NOT NULL) and its value in
       brackets. Saving runs INSERT (for a new record) or UPDATE (of
       the columns that changed); text is quoted with ' doubled, a
       number in an INTEGER column is written as a number. An error
       is shown on the status line and the form stays, to correct it.
       An INTEGER PRIMARY KEY left NULL gets the next number.

       The query screen takes a statement of one line (no ; needed).
       The rows of SELECT, SHOW, DESCRIBE and EXPLAIN are shown in the
       browse grid, read-only; any other statement is run and the
       status line tells how many rows it changed. Up and Down bring
       back earlier statements (the last 20 of the session).

OPTIONS
       -c     make the database (the directory) if it is not there.

KEYS
       Everywhere: ? help (on the query line only while it is empty,
       in the form ^G); ^C leaves sqlui at once, without saving a
       form. The arrow keys, PgUp, PgDn, Home and End are the
       VT100 sequences ESC [ A to D, ESC [ 5 ~, ESC [ 6 ~, ESC [ H or
       1 ~, ESC [ F or 4 ~.

       The tables screen:
              Up Down, k j    choose a table
              Enter, b        browse its rows
              d               describe it
              n               a new table
              x               drop it (asks y or n)
              q               the query screen
              r               read the list again
              Q               quit

       The browse screen:
              Up Down, k j    a row up or down
              PgUp PgDn       a screenful (also ^B ^F, and space)
              Home End, g G   the first or last row
              Left Right      scroll the columns (also h l)
              Enter, e        edit the row in the form
              a               add a row (an empty form)
              D               delete the row (asks y or n)
              /               filter: type a WHERE condition
              o               order: type columns [DESC]
              r               read the rows again
              q, Esc          back

       The record form and the lines typed at the bottom:
              Tab, Down       the next field (^J too)
              Up              the field before
              Left Right      move in the field
              Home End        its start or end (also ^A ^E)
              Backspace       delete the character before
              Del, ^D         delete the character at the cursor
              ^U              clear the field;  ^K  clear to its end
              ^N              make the value NULL (form only)
              Enter, ^S       save the record (Enter on a line:
                              filter, order, statement)
              Esc             cancel

ENVIRONMENT
       LINES, COLUMNS
              the size of the screen (24 and 80 if not set), where
              the system does not know the terminal's (ttysize(3)).
              The size is taken once, when sqlui starts.

EXAMPLES
       sqlui shop            browse and change the database shop
       sqlui -c /tmp/new     a new database, made on the spot

       In the browse screen of a table emp, / then dept = 20 shows
       the rows of department 20; o then salary DESC sorts them.

EXIT STATUS
       0 after Q, 1 after ^C or a signal, 2 if the database cannot
       be opened, the arguments are wrong, or the standard input or
       output is not a terminal.

SEE ALSO
       sql(1), sqldb(3)

BUGS
       A row is found again for UPDATE and DELETE by its PRIMARY KEY;
       in a table without one, by all of its values, so rows that are
       the same in every column cannot be told apart: sqlui says how
       many there are and asks before changing or deleting them all.
       A row edited or deleted by another process in the meantime is
       not noticed until r.

       Browsing with an order (or a query with ORDER BY or GROUP BY)
       sorts the whole result again for every screenful; on a big
       table this is slow and needs memory for all of it.

       An Esc is told from the start of an arrow key's sequence only
       by what arrives with it in one read; on a very slow line an
       arrow key may now and then be taken as Esc.

       A query's grid shows at most 64 columns; a statement is one
       line of at most 1000 characters. The terminal must understand
       VT100 cursor addressing, erase to the end of the line, reverse
       video and bold.
